{"connection_id": "BC-65b4bf04ac8918316ab8", "ref": "1 Clement 7.6", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Noah’s preaching and the hearers saved", "note": "Clement presents Noah as preaching repentance and says those who heeded were saved; this early Christian retelling makes the outcome of a received admonition explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-83d19274567971e6d9ad", "ref": "1 Clement 7.7", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Clement’s Jonah as a model of heeded warning", "note": "Clement recalls that Jonah preached destruction to Nineveh and that the city repented and obtained pardon; this early Christian retelling reads the scriptural warning as a summons whose reception changes the announced outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7bd125078ed1d420d56a", "ref": "Chrysostom, Homilies on Matthew 44", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "The soils as different kinds of hearers", "note": "Chrysostom’s homily on the sower reads the soils as differing hearers of the word; this Christian interpretation develops why one proclamation bears fruit in some and not in others, much like the commentary’s two responses to reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-037bfc08193f8c4de622", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.1.18", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "One proclamation, opposed receptions", "note": "Paul says the word of the cross is foolishness to those perishing but God’s power to those being saved; one proclamation divides hearers by reception and outcome, like the commentary’s two responses."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71a72001b1c270e70a28", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.1.18", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one message, perishing and saving hearers", "note": "The word of the cross is foolishness to those perishing and God's power to those being saved. Its divided audience gives a Christian message-and-recipient pattern, though its proclamation is not identical with the Qur'anic reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ad255559ba9d0c3d1e23", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.7", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God as giver of growth beyond the teacher", "note": "Neither planter nor waterer is decisive, but God who gives growth. This reframes the commentary's reminder-giver limit through the Christian image of teachers who act without controlling the result."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d4cec1000dfebf1215d0", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.4.17", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Timothy sent to remind communities", "note": "Paul sends Timothy to remind the Corinthians of Paul's ways in Christ, giving a mediated New Testament instance of a reminder passed from one person to another."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ca970b160e75832765d9", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.7.5", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mutual abstinence for prayer", "note": "Paul permits spouses temporary, agreed abstinence in order to devote themselves to prayer. This is a close Christian prayer-and-sexuality parallel, but frames abstention as voluntary and temporary rather than an automatic ritual bar."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8b7ba728a4c86e8f9075", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.4.6", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Knowing God and hearing his messengers", "note": "John says whoever knows God listens to the apostolic witnesses, while one not from God does not listen; this directly ties knowledge to receptivity, complementing the commentary’s discussion of knowledge and fear."}
{"connection_id": "BC-37d6bedcf616b7702598", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.2.13", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "receiving apostolic speech as God's word", "note": "The Thessalonians receive the word heard from messengers as God's word, which works in believers; it gives a concrete positive response to a delivered message."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ebd9e3b8d96d4080e64e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.4.16", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "teaching that saves teacher and hearers", "note": "Persistent attention to life and teaching is said to save both the teacher and those who hear, making the possible benefit of instruction explicitly bilateral."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ec94a67c449fda2ed7f0", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.2.15", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The message among those saved and perishing", "note": "Paul calls the apostolic mission Christ’s fragrance among both those being saved and those perishing; the shared proclamation reaches audiences whose outcomes differ, adding a Christian account of divided reception."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e969d62b19e737afb76d", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.2.16", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A fragrance from death or from life", "note": "Paul specifies that the same fragrance is for one person from death to death and for another from life to life; this sharpens the commentary’s contrast between the person who receives the reminder and the one who avoids it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-da0ba4ff6c4c14a4a633", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.4", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Failure to see as imposed blindness", "note": "Paul says the god of this age has blinded unbelievers’ minds so they cannot see the gospel’s light; this gives a different account of failed reception from the commentary’s active image of the wretched person avoiding the reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cd6015e445687907e5c0", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.1.12", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "intentional reminder to those who already know", "note": "The author intends continually to remind hearers of things they already know and in which they stand established; it directly supports the commentary's definition of reminder as a deliberate act toward others."}
{"connection_id": "BC-408b7801bb85dbd8934f", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.2.25", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gentle correction, repentance, knowledge", "note": "The teacher corrects opponents with gentleness in hope that God grants repentance leading to knowledge of truth; this closely accompanies the commentary's gentle address to Pharaoh and its fear-knowledge nexus."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2a60cca4158d4973f0cc", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.3.16", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Scripture profitable for teaching and reproof", "note": "Scripture is called profitable for teaching, reproof, correction, and training, giving a direct Christian formulation of the reminder's possible usefulness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-78c8a70472d488be092c", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.4.2", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The preacher’s duty to reprove and exhort", "note": "Timothy is charged to proclaim the word and to reprove, rebuke, and exhort with patience; this gives a concrete Christian formulation of the admonisher’s task even when response varies."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b840b0b9b8f0dbfd23ce", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.4.2", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "preaching, reproof, rebuke, and exhortation", "note": "The charge to proclaim the word includes reproof, rebuke, and exhortation with patient teaching, a direct Christian description of the outward act the commentary calls reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f33b888af55ca4573217", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.4.3", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A hearer who will not endure sound teaching", "note": "Paul says people will not endure sound teaching and will gather teachers to suit their desires; this describes avoidance as actively selecting away from admonition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-021280a9165314678870", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.4.4", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Turning the ears from truth", "note": "Paul says they will turn their ears away from truth and toward myths; the bodily image of turning away closely specifies the commentary’s image of keeping the reminder at a distance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7f1e5e65865b1205be8e", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.4.4", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "turning hearing away from truth", "note": "Those described turn their hearing away from the truth and turn aside to myths, a verbal and spatially shaped counterpart to the recipient who avoids the reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5798e4e0a0fadd182a1e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.13.46", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Paul says his hearers thrust aside the word", "note": "Paul and Barnabas say their audience judged itself unworthy of eternal life by thrusting aside God’s word, so they turn to the Gentiles; this concretely depicts a hearer’s active rejection of the announcement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5e0c2c422f7e4339bbf3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.13.46", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "rejecting God's word and its consequence", "note": "Paul tells his audience that, by thrusting aside God's word, they judge themselves unworthy of eternal life. This is a missionary scene in which refusal of the offered word has a declared result."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cc05a6f4b53c44993903", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.13.48", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Gentile hearers rejoice and believe", "note": "The Gentiles rejoice when they hear Paul’s message and believe; this supplies the receptive counterpart in the same proclamation scene."}

{"connection_id": "BC-f60545fae85a09da5bd0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.32", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Mockery and postponement after Paul’s announcement", "note": "In Athens, some mock when Paul speaks of resurrection, while others say they will hear him again; the scene records distinct responses to a public religious summons."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8e0a3bf06dff65989d6f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.34", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Some hearers join and believe", "note": "The following verse names people who join Paul and believe; it completes the same scene with the response of hearers who accept the proclamation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-af77db4de7f4259ca2d3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.28.24", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Different responses to Paul’s explanation", "note": "After Paul explains the kingdom of God and argues from Moses and the prophets, some are persuaded and others disbelieve; this is a clear scene of one teaching meeting divided responses."}
{"connection_id": "BC-78e9af0b7e006b81034e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.28.27", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Isaiah’s hard-hearing prophecy applied to Paul’s audience", "note": "Paul quotes Isaiah’s dull ears and closed eyes to explain why some hearers will not understand, turn, and be healed; this Christian continuation shows an interpretive tradition applying Isaiah’s account of failed reception to a new audience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e320fd81a4adec1c6c29", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.51", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Resisting the Spirit as the ancestors did", "note": "Stephen accuses his hearers of always resisting the Holy Spirit, as their ancestors did; this gives rejection of divine speech a repeated communal history."}
{"connection_id": "BC-95b9bf9b7639093422ac", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.16", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "teaching and admonishing one another", "note": "The indwelling word leads the community to teach and admonish one another in wisdom, preserving the commentary's specifically interpersonal account of reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-05424f8ef6b4235b9c6d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.25", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Do not refuse the one speaking", "note": "Hebrews warns its readers not to refuse the one speaking from heaven and recalls that those who refused the earthly warning did not escape; it explicitly joins refusal of admonition with judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dfa4022f90be0e2daf54", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.25", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "refusing the one who speaks", "note": "The warning not to refuse the one who speaks preserves the commentary's agency distinction: a divine word is offered, and refusal is the hearer's perilous response."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1058e7c41ab2171be8bc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.2.1", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Pay closer attention lest hearers drift away", "note": "Hebrews urges readers to pay closer attention to what they heard so they do not drift away from it; this makes avoidance a gradual slipping from an already heard warning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-54663e9eb7889458f10d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.2.1", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "attention to what was heard and drifting away", "note": "Hebrews calls for closer attention to what has been heard lest hearers drift away; the nautical image supplies a concrete Christian counterpart to turning aside from a reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-889c8b8c11f4de82015b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.2.3", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Neglecting salvation has no escape", "note": "Hebrews asks how anyone can escape if they neglect such a great salvation; this adds the consequence of letting a saving announcement pass by."}
{"connection_id": "BC-119e96f31743fb6ec28c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.3.13", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Daily exhortation against a hardened heart", "note": "Hebrews tells the community to exhort one another every day so no one is hardened by sin’s deceit; admonition is a mutual practice meant to keep hearers receptive."}
{"connection_id": "BC-86fd72013db67a2749af", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.3.13", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "exhort one another against hardening", "note": "The community is to exhort one another daily so that none is hardened by sin's deceit; it makes repeated mutual reminder the antidote to the inward condition that refuses a divine word."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4188ab18506222058e6d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.3.15", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hearing without hardening", "note": "Hebrews repeats, “Today, if you hear his voice, do not harden your hearts”; this directly joins hearing a divine summons to the choice between receiving it and resisting it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-73452a8348796ba041d7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.4.2", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The message that did not benefit its hearers", "note": "Hebrews says the good news heard by the wilderness generation did not benefit them because it was not joined with faith; this closely parallels the commentary’s question of whether the reminder benefits and makes faith the interpretive bridge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2de8a3fe832eb4e9c457", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.4.2", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word heard but not beneficial", "note": "Hebrews says the word heard did not benefit its earlier hearers because it was not joined with faith, an unusually exact New Testament articulation of the commentary's conditional benefit of reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2b073cc6574fbfe5fd9f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.21", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Receive the implanted word that can save", "note": "James tells readers to put away wickedness and receive meekly the implanted word, which can save their souls; this makes admonition a saving gift that must be welcomed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-be738f209d006612e7d3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.21", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "receiving the implanted word", "note": "James commands hearers to receive the implanted word, which is able to save them; its reception-versus-rejection logic makes the benefit of a delivered word explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-acaecd23196d9e9bd816", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.22", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Doers rather than hearers only", "note": "James warns readers not to be hearers only who deceive themselves, but to do the word; this distinguishes exposure to instruction from receiving it in practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-830974f02db9af15dd1b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.25", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Doing the heard word brings blessing", "note": "James describes the person who looks into the law and perseveres as a doer who will be blessed in doing; it specifies the benefit of receiving instruction as practice, not mere hearing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7fc46f976d0d067914d2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.19", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Bringing a wanderer back from error", "note": "James says whoever brings a person wandering from truth back saves that person from death and covers many sins; this gives the reminder a neighbor-directed, restorative action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-77db9b675a1fdccf26dc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.19", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one person bringing back a wanderer", "note": "James imagines a person wandering from truth and another bringing that person back, an interpersonal counterpart to reminder aimed at someone who has moved away."}
{"connection_id": "BC-daa97fc46ef3529656ec", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.20", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "turning a sinner back from wandering", "note": "The one who turns a sinner from wandering saves a soul from death and covers sins, making the benefit of successful corrective return concrete."}
{"connection_id": "BC-20a5244e8554429132e3", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.12.48", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rejected words become the judge", "note": "Jesus says the one who rejects him and does not receive his words has a judge: the word he spoke will judge on the last day; it connects non-reception to the judgment that follows."}
{"connection_id": "BC-203c7a72cd9e2ac684fc", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.3.20", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "evil-doer avoiding the light", "note": "The evil-doer hates the light and does not come to it lest deeds be exposed. Its avoidance is more active than verbal contradiction, clarifying the commentary's spatial account of shunning admonition."}

{"connection_id": "BC-c60acd30fa6103f0afa3", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.24", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hearing and believing lead from death to life", "note": "Jesus says the one who hears his word and believes the Father has eternal life and has passed from death to life; the verse links reception to a changed fate."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a4a0146994e0e770a4c1", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.40", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Refusing to come for life", "note": "Jesus tells his interlocutors that they are unwilling to come to him to have life; this is a direct instance of refusing the offered source of life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-04aeb6484e412b0f0b5c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.10.16", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hearing or rejecting the commissioned messenger", "note": "Jesus says that hearing his envoys is hearing him, while rejecting them reaches through to the sender. It clarifies the commentary's distinction between the reminder-giver and the addressee responsible for reception."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9babca533c4463e99c98", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.11.28", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Blessing belongs to those who keep the word", "note": "Jesus says blessed are those who hear God’s word and keep it; this pairs reception with obedience and the blessed outcome that contrasts with the commentary’s unhappy avoider."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ee9d946aa7f97e8b76c9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.11.28", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "blessed hearers and keepers of God's word", "note": "Jesus calls those who hear God's word and keep it blessed, supplying the positive happy pole beside the commentary's fearful receiver and its contrast with the most wretched."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c748152f2aecd6e8dc26", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.31", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Refusal of Moses and the Prophets", "note": "In the rich man’s story, Abraham says that those who do not hear Moses and the Prophets will not be persuaded even if someone rises from the dead; the warning cannot be made effective by a more spectacular sign when prior admonition is refused."}
{"connection_id": "BC-701fee964176babd4702", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.8.15", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The word held fast and made fruitful", "note": "Jesus says good-soil hearers hold the word fast in an honest and good heart and bear fruit with patience; this specifies an enduring form of the beneficial reception described in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5d61c697b832848b81c8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.8.18", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Take care how you hear", "note": "Jesus warns his hearers to consider how they hear, since the one who has will receive more and the one who lacks will lose even what he thinks he has; this makes the consequences of response explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-06debc13b0a47899ad49", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.10.49", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus stops for a blind petitioner", "note": "Jesus stops and has the blind Bartimaeus called to him. Beside the commentary's rebuke of a prophet who turned from a blind seeker, it offers a controlled comparison of a religious teacher's response to a blind petitioner, not the same story."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d6ff4cea136fc6ff2acb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.4.16", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Joyful reception that does not last", "note": "In the sower parable, rocky-ground hearers receive the word at once with joy but fall away when trouble comes; this preserves a secondary case beyond simple acceptance or avoidance, where initial reception fails to endure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-906b159028b6af6abe96", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.4.20", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hearing, accepting, and bearing fruit", "note": "In the sower parable, good soil represents those who hear the word, accept it, and bear fruit; this supplies a specific picture of a reminder that benefits its hearer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1ee1f30d09f4e82d7409", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.4.20", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hearing, accepting, and bearing fruit", "note": "The good-soil hearers hear the word, accept it, and bear fruit; this gives a concrete Gospel image for a reminder whose reception has beneficial effect."}
{"connection_id": "BC-186a3d56433e2cdeef1a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.10.14", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "nonreception of messenger and words", "note": "Where people neither receive the envoy nor hear the words, disciples leave and shake dust from their feet. It supplies a narrative counterpart to the commentary's limited role for the admonisher."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ffed2a83876599b05553", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.13.15", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Closed ears and an unhealed hearer", "note": "Matthew’s quotation of Isaiah describes a people whose heart has grown dull and ears are hard, so they do not hear, understand, turn, and receive healing; it gives a scriptural account of refusal as blocked reception."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c6dae0c8ba6fb95296ff", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.13.16", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Disciples blessed with seeing and hearing", "note": "Jesus tells his disciples their eyes and ears are blessed because they see and hear; this supplies the positive counterpart to the dull hearing in the preceding verse and to the commentary’s fear-led recipient."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f0ae645b4e2fbb244446", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.15", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "correction and \"if he hears you\"", "note": "Private reproof is directed to a brother, and hearing the reprover gains that brother; it concretely pairs transitive admonition with a beneficial response."}
{"connection_id": "BC-554c884f6b86220bb9fc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.23.37", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jerusalem is unwilling to be gathered", "note": "Jesus says he often wanted to gather Jerusalem’s children as a hen gathers her brood, but they were unwilling; the image makes refusal of a protective summons concrete."}
{"connection_id": "BC-57658e1a36cc1fa04e9c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.24", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hearing and doing the sayings of Jesus", "note": "Jesus compares the one who hears his words and does them to a house built on rock; it gives a concrete outcome for receiving instruction rather than merely encountering it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5bd413b8286c5bebd8f5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.24", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hearing Jesus' words and doing them", "note": "The hearer who acts on Jesus' words is the wise house-builder; this gives an enacted Christian counterpart to beneficial reception of admonition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ce7278292b3b1925d57", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.26", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hearing without doing", "note": "Jesus contrasts the person who hears his words but does not do them with the wise builder; the foolish house’s collapse makes the consequence of non-reception concrete."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6a3f38566ab5d2daf53a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.26", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hearing without doing", "note": "The person who hears Jesus' words but does not act is the foolish house-builder, a distinct outcome for external hearing without the response commended in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-036839d169dd1646a90e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.3.19", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Reproof as loving discipline", "note": "The risen Christ says he reproves and disciplines those he loves, then urges repentance; this Christian text interprets correction as care and gives the admonition a restorative purpose."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c2b7dd64340082da4f3d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.10.21", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God’s outstretched hands to a disobedient people", "note": "Paul quotes God’s all-day outstretched hands toward a disobedient and contrary people; the image pairs persistent invitation with refusal and gives a Christian rereading of Israel’s resistance to divine speech."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6587c66eb585d676c1f9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Titus.3.10", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "first and second warning to a divisive person", "note": "After a first and second warning, the community is to decline further association with a divisive person. It gives a concrete Christian boundary for correction after a recipient proves unreceptive."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b48c18d429865182d292", "ref": "Bavli Shabbat 54b", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "responsibility to protest wrongdoing", "note": "The Bavli holds a person accountable for failing to protest wrongdoing within the reach of that person's influence. It is a Jewish extension of the duty to address another, complementing but not collapsing the commentary's limit on the reminder-giver."}

{"connection_id": "BC-1e9bb7a1a767464fc42e", "ref": "Bavli Yevamot 65b", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "admonition that will be heeded", "note": "The Bavli's maxim that one should say a matter that will be heard and should not say one that will not be heard is a direct Jewish interpretive counterpart to the conditional \"if the reminder benefits.\""}
{"connection_id": "BC-10b7b3ce9cd4c3307fa3", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 3:2", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Fearers reinterpreted as paired Torah learners", "note": "The Mishnah reads Malachi’s people who fear God and speak together as two who sit with words of Torah between them, with the divine presence among them; this rabbinic interpretation turns fearful reception into shared study, echoing the commentary’s description of admonition as passing to another."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c6cc88d78b5c871abba9", "ref": "Mishnah Berakhot 3:4", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "baal qeri and recitation of Shema", "note": "The mishnah limits a man with a seminal emission to inward reflection on the Shema rather than its normal verbal recitation. It provides a Jewish liturgical analogue to the commentary's association of the postcoital state with distance from prayer, without equating their rules."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6361b9298c09c7c5ce0e", "ref": "Sirach 1:14", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Fear as wisdom’s beginning", "note": "Ben Sira says fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom; alongside the commentary’s account of fear as reverent and associated with knowledge, this Jewish wisdom text makes fear the entry into wisdom rather than its result."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d903bc98822b71a3c63", "ref": "Sirach 2:15", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Those who fear the Lord do not disobey his words", "note": "Ben Sira says those who fear the Lord will not disobey his words and those who love him keep his ways; this directly links reverent fear with receiving and following instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1262c5b933845729a871", "ref": "Sirach 6:33", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Listening yields knowledge", "note": "Ben Sira says the one who loves listening gains knowledge and the attentive person becomes wise; this gives a concise account of instruction benefiting a receptive hearer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-347008fb597844882a88", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.12.14", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Fear expressed by hearing and serving", "note": "Samuel tells Israel that if they fear YHWH, serve him, and listen to his voice rather than rebel, they and their king will fare well; it directly links fear to taking divine instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cb967bb8a916135994d0", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.12.15", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Refusal brings the Lord’s hand against them", "note": "Samuel warns that if Israel will not listen to YHWH’s voice and rebels against his command, the Lord’s hand will be against them; this gives the non-hearing side its consequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6a0755df7b17cedce405", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.15.22", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Obedience is better than sacrifice", "note": "Samuel tells Saul that listening to YHWH’s voice is better than sacrifice; the rebuke makes heedful response itself the required offering, not a religious substitute for it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-94f1ac32a035277f5ac5", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.15.23", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rejecting the word brings rejection", "note": "Samuel says Saul rejected YHWH’s word, so YHWH rejected him as king; this states the consequence of a ruler who hears the divine command but will not receive it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c4906949fe9b196b4c7", "ref": "WLC:2Chr.36.16", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Despising words until judgment", "note": "The chronicler says the people mocked God’s messengers, despised his words, and scoffed at his prophets until wrath came; it is a concentrated account of rejecting repeated admonition and its outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-976f7506b404074f7db1", "ref": "WLC:2Kgs.22.11", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Josiah hears the book and tears his clothes", "note": "When the king hears the words of the book of the law, he tears his clothes; the scene offers a concrete example of a hearer registering a warning rather than passing it by."}
{"connection_id": "BC-78c1d3927d77bb745955", "ref": "WLC:2Kgs.22.19", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A humbled response to heard words", "note": "Huldah says Josiah’s heart was tender and he humbled himself when he heard the words against Jerusalem, so God heard him; this makes inward responsiveness and its consequence explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e03c6fc03243a5f11457", "ref": "WLC:Deut.23.11", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Nocturnal emission and exclusion from camp", "note": "The commentary’s Arabic source note links junub with distance from prayer and the mosque; Deuteronomy orders a man with a nocturnal emission outside the camp until evening, a specific ritual-distance comparison, though its setting is the military camp and it says nothing about prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5d1f92df9a6d3295cd43", "ref": "WLC:Deut.23.12", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Washing and return at evening", "note": "Deuteronomy continues that the man washes and returns to camp at sunset; this clarifies the temporary boundary behind the commentary’s junub association while differing from its prayer-and-mosque framing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-007833e0fc0990c2aec0", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.19", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Choosing life rather than death", "note": "Moses sets life and death before Israel and urges them to choose life; this gives a specific choice-and-consequence frame alongside the commentary’s contrast between the wretched and the opposite of happiness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-09a5f41b017d0dc2b5f3", "ref": "WLC:Deut.31.12", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "public Torah reading, hearing, fear, observance", "note": "The commanded assembly is to hear, learn, fear YHWH, and observe Torah; its ordered response closely matches instruction received by one who fears."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3caf683b17d513538f0e", "ref": "WLC:Deut.4.10", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hearing in order to learn fear", "note": "Moses recalls the assembly at Horeb so Israel would hear God’s words and learn to fear him throughout life, then teach their children; hearing instruction is explicitly the path to the reverent fear the commentary associates with receiving the reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d23eecacc94bc02d5c46", "ref": "WLC:Deut.4.10", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "assembled hearing, learning, fear, teaching", "note": "At Horeb the people are gathered to hear God's words, learn to fear him, and teach their children, giving a communal scriptural form to the commentary's reminder that passes from speaker to hearer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0209b67df22616d429dd", "ref": "WLC:Deut.5.29", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hearing, fear, obedience, and well-being", "note": "After Israel hears God’s voice at Horeb, YHWH wishes they would keep a heart to fear him and his commands always, so it would go well with them and their children; this directly joins heard instruction, reverent fear, and benefit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eb22d0a03e1c92c7b223", "ref": "WLC:Deut.5.29", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "heart that fears and obedience for good", "note": "YHWH wishes Israel had a heart to fear him and keep his commands so that it would go well with them; this joins reverential fear, reception of instruction, and benefit in one appeal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bfc4d8a7a09a1fc25246", "ref": "WLC:Deut.8.2", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ذ ك ر and Hebrew זכר", "note": "The commentary labels the Arabic source root ذ ك ر and frames dhikrā as admonition; Hebrew זכר (zakar, “remember”) is cognate, and Moses uses it to command Israel to remember the wilderness road as a lesson in humility and keeping the commandments. The connection is the remembered lesson, not an equation of memory with admonition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7f1dee77466fa2cdb11b", "ref": "WLC:Deut.8.5", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Discipline understood as a father’s instruction", "note": "Moses tells Israel to know in its heart that YHWH disciplines it as a father disciplines a son; this offers a Jewish scriptural frame in which correction itself is instruction, though it does not describe the audience’s response to a spoken reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-90bf1d0f47fd4d04dcde", "ref": "WLC:Exod.10.3", "target": "sec17", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses confronts Pharaoh’s refusal", "note": "Moses and Aaron ask Pharaoh how long he will refuse to humble himself before YHWH and demand Israel’s release; this is the same ruler’s rejection of a divine summons, in the biblical narrative’s own terms."}
